I propose to use the following table structure:

{code:sql}CREATE TABLE `event_log`
(
    `id`              bigint(20) unsigned NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
    `anonymous_id`    varchar(255)        NOT NULL,
    `entity_id`       bigint(20) unsigned          DEFAULT NULL,
    `entity_type`     varchar(255)        NOT NULL,
    `event_name`      varchar(255)        NOT NULL,
    `event_timestamp` timestamp           NOT NULL,
    `attributes`      longtext,
    `created`         timestamp           NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    `last_update`     timestamp           NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    PRIMARY KEY (`id`)
) ENGINE = InnoDB
  DEFAULT CHARSET = utf8{code}

Naming can be discussed.

I’m thinking about adding {{entity_type}} as enum, as we have a small number of potential candidates for logging. They are: {{driver_registration}}, {{driver}} (possibly, {{fleet_owner_registration}}, {{fleet_owner}}).

But regarding {{event_name}} (or {{event_type}}) – for better flexibility, I wouldn’t use enums here. Maybe in future, we can have a separate table with event names/types that we want to track, and we can extend this list via Admin.

An idea behind having {{anonymous_id}} is that we don’t have anything on the landing page to link with lead. So, we generate this {{anonymous_id}} on the client-side, store the first record (or records) of {{event_log}} with this ID but without {{entity_id}}. Then, as soon as we get a real id from DB for this entity (in our case — {{driver_registration_log_id}}), we can link them together and populate the rest of the {{null}} values for {{event_log}}.

Questions:

