The possible implementation is quite hacky - we would need to add a route {{/}} for each {{virtualhost}} like in the example below:

{noformat}+       // MethodsRegex list
+       MethodsRegex = `(GET|HEAD|OPTIONS|POST|PUT)`

+// blackholeRoute helper function generates route for blocking restricted HTTP methods
+func blackholeRoute(methodsRegex string) *route.Route {
+       return &route.Route{
+               Name: "blackhole",
+               Match: &route.RouteMatch{
+                       PathSpecifier: &route.RouteMatch_Prefix{Prefix: "/"},
+                       Headers: []*route.HeaderMatcher{
+                               {
+                                       Name: ":method",
+                                       HeaderMatchSpecifier: &route.HeaderMatcher_SafeRegexMatch{
+                                               SafeRegexMatch: &matcher.RegexMatcher{
+                                                       EngineType: &matcher.RegexMatcher_GoogleRe2{
+                                                               GoogleRe2: &matcher.RegexMatcher_GoogleRE2{},
+                                                       },
+                                                       Regex: methodsRegex,
+                                               },
+                                       },
+                                       InvertMatch: true,
+                               },
+                       },
+               },
+               Action: &route.Route_DirectResponse{
+                       DirectResponse: &route.DirectResponseAction{
+                               Status: 405,
+                       },
+               },
+       }
+}{noformat}

This rule will block all the requests with the HTTP method not equal to listed in {{MethodsRegex}}. Also, I’ve found out that Envoy with block all the requests with non-RFC methods, which was the primary concern. So we agreed to reject this task as the default Envoy behaviour fits our needs.

